* StrFormat explicitly doesn't need size prefixes since it does C++
  magic and knows exact argument type. Like Go. So drop those 'll'-s
  and explicit casts all over the code when StrFormat-ting.

* why CheckExitState is barking on !at_function_entry without really checking the state ?

* Disassembler::DecodeOne should probably not use ptr to itself and
  risk clobbering instr_ after unrelated decodes (e.g. in
  ResolveJumpTable etc). Have it decode into &out parameter and
  returns true/false. Rename it as well into Decode to avoid any
  confusion.

* pending_pushes stuff is wrong (decrement instead of drop in the
  drain loop). just make hash of addr -> bool instead. and keep the
  entries for efficiency, but set to false on dequeue

* consider some sort of reserve call for all kinds of containers in
  the Check. Slight efficiency win.

* double check if say storing {x,y,z}mm field over set of slots is
  clobbring them. Probably not.

* AbsState::SetSlot erases any is_top() value, which drops the bounds a
  kTop-with-bounds carries -- so spilling a movzbl result and reloading it
  loses the width fact and any proven guard folded into it. Pre-existing,
  and the 400-binary sweep says it currently costs nothing measurable, but
  it is a silent precision cliff if compilers start spilling switch indices
  more.
